{
  "method_label": "Method 4: North American manually guided backbone\u2013feeder construction",
  "method_description": "Method 4 removes the previous corridor-access-anchor experiment, so feeder trees and fallback spokes connect only to fixed hubs rather than to arbitrary intermediate backbone points. This update adds Birmingham, Nashville, Charlotte, Louisville, Oklahoma City, Kansas City, Omaha, and St. Louis as eastern/central structural hubs, removes Virginia Beach after visual review, and replaces the older direct Chicago\u2013Atlanta and Atlanta\u2013Washington DC links with a decomposed eastern hub chain to test whether denser fixed hubs reduce messy fallback access in the eastern US. The method adds Canada through a deliberately simple manually guided structure: one west-east trunk from Vancouver through Calgary, Winnipeg, Toronto, Montreal, and Quebec City, plus cross-border corridors from Vancouver to Seattle, Calgary to the US network via Montana and Wyoming, Winnipeg to the US network through the Red River Valley, Twin Cities, southern Minnesota, and Wisconsin, Toronto to Chicago via Detroit, Toronto to New York via Hamilton, Buffalo, Rochester, Syracuse, and Albany, and Montreal to New York via Boston and New England. Albuquerque is added as a fixed Southwest interchange hub, with Denver routed south to Albuquerque, and Albuquerque connected onward to Phoenix and Dallas. Pittsburgh is added as a fixed Appalachian/Great Lakes interchange hub, Boston is added as a fixed New England hub, and Philadelphia is added as a fixed hub on the DC\u2013NYC corridor. The Canada boundary is cached and simplified to speed up repeated runs. Salt Lake City\u2013Denver is left without forced waypoints so the road-routing algorithm can choose its own path. The targeted structural fixes and Mississippi gap-fill cleanup is retained; the previous redundant-gap-fill pruning experiment is not applied. Fallback spokes are repaired after feeder-tree construction: where possible, old direct fallback spokes are replaced by short access links to named city/interchange anchors that already lie on the built network. Phoenix remains a fixed Southwest hub, Los Angeles\u2013San Antonio remains split through Phoenix, and Key West is removed from the terminal set.",
